Add a wait and a conditional loop. This version increments immediately when Interact is pressed, then repeats while held.

The original runs only once because `Ongoing - Each Player` checks whether the conditions have **become** true. While you keep holding Interact, the condition remains true; it must become false and then true again to trigger another execution. [Ongoing - Each Player](https://workshop.codes/wiki/articles/4824)

This complete native example uses a separate `Ticks` player variable for each player, the held-button condition, and an addition of 1. [Variables](https://workshop.codes/wiki/articles/2080), [Is Button Held](https://workshop.codes/wiki/articles/4638), [Modify Player Variable](https://workshop.codes/wiki/articles/4396)

```text
variables
{
    player:
        0: Ticks
}

rule("Count while Interact is held")
{
    event
    {
        Ongoing - Each Player;
        All;
        All;
    }

    conditions
    {
        Is Button Held(Event Player, Button(Interact)) == True;
    }

    actions
    {
        Modify Player Variable(Event Player, Ticks, Add, 1);
        Wait(0.100, Abort When False);
        Loop If Condition Is True;
    }
}
```

`Abort When False` stops the action list if you release Interact during the wait. After a completed wait, `Loop If Condition Is True` returns to the first action only while the rule's condition remains true. The wait also satisfies the loop's required pause. Pressing Interact again starts another run; this rule does not reset the count. [Wait Behaviour](https://workshop.codes/wiki/articles/4854), [Loop If Condition Is True](https://workshop.codes/wiki/articles/4392)

Timing caveat: the archive documents that `Wait(0.100, ...)` is rounded up to 0.112 seconds by the server, so this requests a 0.1-second interval rather than guaranteeing exact 100 ms timing. [Wait](https://workshop.codes/wiki/articles/4480)

